>Fair and not fair are arbitrary terms.  You need something to base them
>on.  

No you don't really. That's the point that I'm really contesting, I
don't think we need something to base what's fair and not fair on, I
think people can judge that for themselves.

Not everything can be easily quantified, and at the end of the day my
opinion on this subject is: 'if it looks like a duck, smells like a
duck, quacks like a duck, it's probably a duck'. 

If something looks like it's cheating, it's cheating, you don't really
need to break it down into 'abuse' and 'cheating'. 

Creating infinite wishes by dropping lodestones is (was) cheating. We
don't need to make up a term for what it is, it's cheating, plain and
simple. 

Renaming objects to avoid cursing isn't cheating, it's just stupid and
if you want to not be stupid you won't do it. You can't quantify it,
because nobody is going to say you're naughty because you carry around
a pet gem named 'bob' yet you're doing the same thing. 

It comes down to intention, and at that level we escape quantification
and have to simply accept responsibility for playing the game in a
reasonable manner.

I guess in a way 'abusive' is an apt term, but I really just don't
think it's something that deserves a precise definition when all it
really is is 'people doing shit when they damn well know better'. 

